 to actually receive the thing keep it  cold and give it to people  yeah we do we've already identified the  10 places uh  in ohio the hospitals uh we're  going to identify in the next several  days for the federal government all the  other places where the shipments are  supposed to be made  for the smaller counties we may actually  break those out and our national guard  may be involved in getting those out as  well so we're  we're moving along and we're we're  excited you know that  december 15th date uh just can't come  soon enough we're very excited about it  and very happy about it it's absolutely  exciting it's also  tricky and the stakes are are high at  one point robert redford the  cdc chief said that six billion dollars  would be needed by the states to  facilitate this distribution but only a  couple hundred million had been  have been put out there you have all the  money you need to pay the people you  need to to get these vaccines  everywhere in the state no we do not but  we're going to get it out uh one way or  the other we have the ability to do that  what we would like of course is a is a  bill from congress uh  you know you carried a story this  morning about negotiations continuing on  and  you know i'm still hopeful that  democrats and republicans and the  president can all get together  uh and get a bill passed because we we  really need that we need to be able to  roll the money over for example that's  already been  been sent out uh we need the flexibility  there  our local governments need some money  our schools need money so  help me understand much like a bill if  you if you don't have the money  to pay the people to distribute the  vaccine how are you going to do it  we're going to do it i mean we have  cares act dollars that we can use we  also have  you know we're going to do whatever we  have to do to get it done  but the bigger picture i think is that  congress does need to pass another bill  for  for many many reasons and i'm hopeful  that that will happen  governor dwight no no no one should ohio  should think that if congress doesn't  pass a bill we're not going to get this  out though i will make that very clear  we will get it out and we'll start the  shots  just as soon as we get it there's a lot  of skepticism  in some parts of the country about the  vaccine it's safety whether people will  be willing to take it that's a big  obstacle  what's your plan in the state of ohio to  convince skeptical populations that this  is a good thing  well i think the most important thing is  is what  medical doctors say and you know one of  the things that we've tried to do  is throughout the pandemic particularly  in the last month or so is to have  frontline nurses and doctors  talking directly to the people of the  state i think they're probably more  believable than  the governor and other politicians so  that's who's going to be  talking to the people about this because  you know as dr fauci pointed out  yesterday  you know everything has been followed  the normal  the protocol it's been quick but  there's no shortcuts that have been  taken and that people will be able to  feel confident that they can take these  these  this vaccine and i think that message  though  comes not from politicians that message  comes from  doctors and medical experts who who  understand  what went on in regard to the  development of this vaccine i think  those facts are going to come out more  in the next several weeks  i hope people hear them governor dewine  i have to ask you about the impeachment  articles filed officially against you  yesterday  accusing you of callous disregard uh for  the businesses and the people affected  by the restrictions you've passed  in an effort to save lives what's your  response  to those articles well look it's four  member  four members of the general assembly  they introduced a resolution before to  have me impeached i guess they have a  right to go and file anything they want  to  